--- a/.kiro/specs/migrate-off-request/design.md
+++ b/.kiro/specs/migrate-off-request/design.md
@@ -52,4 +52,25 @@ What it compares and ignores:
 
 ## Known behavioural differences
 
-_(to be filled in from the séance results; empty until the séance has run)_
+The replacement is `src/vendor/request-lite.js`, a small module the project owns, built on `fetch`. It keeps request's answers wherever `fetch` allows it (see the séance, `seance/request/seance.test.mjs`). The table lists what `fetch` or the replacement does differently. Each row is **accepted**, has its own test in the section "seance: accepted differences" that asserts what request does (while the package is installed) and what the replacement does, and is the only input class the property test's generators leave out.
+
+Matched on purpose (the séance found each as a counterexample of an early version): `qs` encoding (RFC 3986, `*` and `~`, lone surrogates), `json: true` leaving a non-JSON body as text and an empty body as `undefined`, `accept: application/json` and `content-type` headers, credentials in the URL as a Basic `Authorization` header, GET redirects (any 3xx with a `Location`, at most 10, `Referer` set, none for POST), the meaning of `timeout` (a non-number means none, idle time between bytes, error code `ESOCKETTIMEDOUT`), error codes (`ECONNREFUSED`, `ECONNRESET`, `EPROTO`, `ERR_UNESCAPED_CHARACTERS`, `ERR_SOCKET_BAD_PORT`, `ERR_INVALID_CHAR`), a bad URI reaching the callback and a non-serialisable payload being thrown.
+
+| # | Input | request | replacement (fetch) | Why accepted |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | Character U+0080..U+00FF in the base URL path | sends a raw latin1 byte (a strict server answers 400) | sends UTF-8 `%XX` | request's behaviour is invalid HTTP |
+| 2 | `.` or `..` segment in the base URL path | sends it | resolves it | `fetch` normalises URLs; a base URL has no such segments |
+| 3 | `?` directly followed by `#` in the base URL | sends `/p?` | sends `/p` | an empty query is dropped by the URL parser |
+| 4 | Query string in the base URL of `getRate` | merges it with `qs` | throws `TypeError` | porting `qs.parse` is out of proportion; the base URL is configuration. Refusing is loud, not silent |
+| 5 | Something other than `/`, `?`, `#` right after `host:port` | sends a malformed request line | rejects `ERR_INVALID_URL` | invalid configuration either way |
+| 6 | Response status 421 | one request | two requests (`fetch` retries on the reused connection) | cannot be switched off; the service is read-only for GET. **For `archiveReceipt` (POST) a 421 repeats the POST**: review if the archive is not idempotent |
+| 7 | Response status 407 | resolves `{ status: 407 }` | rejects `TypeError` (`fetch` makes it a network error) | cannot be changed; `getRate` rejects either way, `archiveReceipt` now rejects instead of resolving the status |
+| 8 | Response status 205 with a body | returns the body | drops it | a 205 must not have a body |
+| 9 | URL on a port `fetch` blocks (1, 6000, 25, ...) | connects | rejects before connecting | cannot be changed |
+| 10 | Gzip answer | returns the compressed bytes as text | decodes it | `fetch` asks for gzip; a service that compresses only on request gives the same JSON |
+| 11 | Object or array as `from`/`to` | writes `base[a]=1` | throws `TypeError` | the project passes currency codes (strings) |
+| 12 | Negative `timeoutMs` | coin toss (depends on connection reuse) | no timeout | request's own answer is not deterministic |
+
+Ignored in the comparison because the project cannot set or see them: the request headers `accept-encoding`, `accept-language`, `sec-fetch-mode` (added by `fetch`), `host` (carries the port), and `accept: */*` (added by `fetch` when a falsy payload leaves no `Accept` header). The wording of error messages is not compared.
+
+Not covered by the séance: HTTPS to a real certificate, proxies and environment proxy variables (request honours `HTTP_PROXY`; `fetch` does not), cookies, response bodies larger than a few kilobytes, keep-alive and connection pooling effects, timeouts under 50 ms, and options the project does not pass (the replacement refuses them with a `TypeError`). Node 18 may print an experimental-feature warning the first time `fetch` is used (not checked: this machine runs Node 22).
-- 
